# Security Tests

Test each security boundary where user input affects authorization, rendered output, or query construction. A defect at such a boundary can be difficult to detect because the feature may continue to work.

Write a test for each of these cases:

- **Cross-record access.** Request a record owned by a different user. Read `rules/endpoint-tests.twig` for why the response should possibly be `404` rather than `403`.
- **Each unprivileged role.** Use a data provider over the roles that the endpoint must refuse.
- **Escaping user-provided content.** Test escaping in templates, elements, and mail. Include names and every free-text field a template renders. Assert that dangerous characters are escaped and the raw value is absent. Do not assert an exact entity for a quote, because escaping helpers and mail inliners may decode it.
- **Injection into dynamic query components.** Examples include sort columns, filter fields, and sort directions passed to the query builder.
- **An unexpected key** in a payload or entity data. A patch that accepts every key can set an attribute the user must not control — check the entity `$_accessible` whitelist.

```php
public function test_escapes_dangerous_content_on_the_article_page(): void
{
    // Fixture row with title: O'Reilly <script>alert('xss')</script>
    $this->get('/articles/view/1');
    $this->assertResponseOk();
    $this->assertResponseContains('&lt;script&gt;');
    $this->assertResponseNotContains("<script>alert('xss')</script>");
}
```

CakePHP provides defenses against mass assignment, unauthorized access, and unescaped output. Test that the application applies the appropriate defense to each attribute, route, and template.
